<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleftherios Venizelos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleftherios Venizelos was a Cretan politician who led the campaign for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>'s union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and became Prime Minister of Greece in 1910. He doubled the country's territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>. Clashing with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> over entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, he set up a rival government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> in 1916, and led Greece several times until 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos.jpg" alt="Eleftherios Venizelos" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photoprint copyrighted by Harris &amp; Ewing (No known restrictions on publication) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleftherios_Venizelos_1919_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleftherios Kyriakou Venizelos was born on 23 August 1864 at Mournies, near Chania, in Ottoman Crete, the son of the merchant and revolutionary Kyriakos Venizelos. When the Cretan revolution of 1866 broke out, the family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Kythira</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syros">Syros</a>, returning only after an amnesty in 1872. He took a law degree at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-and-kapodistrian-university-of-athens">University of Athens</a> and from 1886 worked as a lawyer in Chania. In December 1891 he married Maria Katelouzou, who died in November 1894 after the birth of their second child; in mourning he grew the beard and moustache he kept for life. He married Helena Schilizzi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in September 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Venizelos entered Cretan politics in the elections of 2 April 1889 as a deputy of the island's liberal party, known for his eloquence and radical opinions. Cretans had risen repeatedly for enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and in the autonomous Cretan State under Prince George of Greece as High Commissioner he became minister of Justice in 1899. He insisted that the constitution make no reference to religion, so that all residents would feel represented, and clashed with the Prince over his promise to seek annexation from the Great Powers. On 10 March 1905 rebels led by Venizelos gathered at Theriso and declared the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> with Greece.</p><p>Invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1909, he became Prime Minister in 1910 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>. His reforms, culminating in the 1911 Constitution, covered labour law, education and the army and navy. He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912 and 1913, after which Greece had doubled its area and population. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, against the neutral policy of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>, producing the National Schism: a pro-Venizelos provisional government in the north confronted the royal government in Athens, and the population stayed polarised between Venizelists and royalists for decades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Venizelos secured territorial concessions in Western Anatolia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> after the war, but lost the election of November 1920, when anti-Venizelists took 246 of 370 seats, and he failed even to win a seat himself. He went into exile and represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the negotiations that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a>. After a landslide in 1928 he governed until 1932, seeking better relations with Greece's neighbours, signing agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1928 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1929, and dealing with refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</p><p>After coups in his support failed, he was sentenced to death in absentia in March 1935 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 18 March 1936. He was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> near Chania. He is often called the Maker of Modern Greece, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> he founded outlived him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleftherios Venizelos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Goudi coup of 1909 <b>Eleftherios Venizelos</b> came from Crete to lead reforming governments that revised the constitution in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince George's autocratic style clashed with <b>Eleftherios Venizelos</b>, who led the Therisos revolt in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/state/cretan-state">Cretan State</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First World War, King Constantine I insisted on neutrality while Prime Minister <b>Eleftherios Venizelos</b> wanted to join the Entente.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-national-defence">Provisional Government of National Defence</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Venizelos</b> government of 1928-1932 brought stability, a peace treaty with Turkey and public works, but the Great Depression ended it.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-hellenic-republic">Second Hellenic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mournies, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.49,24.01&amp;year=1910" aria-label="Mournies on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Eleftherios Venizelos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4584 4521h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4584 4521h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.49,24.01&amp;year=1910">What was here in 1910? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
